contentText:
Miranda Lambert has been cookin&#8217; up new music lately. The most awarded artist in Academy of Country Music Awards history is set to release her new album, Crisco , on Oct. 2 via MCA.

Produced by Lambert and Jesse Frasure, the dozen-track album features the &#8217;70s-inspired groove of the title track, as well as &#8220;A Song to Sing,&#8221; Lambert&#8217;s Grammy-nominated duet with Chris Stapleton.

Elsewhere, songs such as &#8220;Two Things Can Be True&#8221; and &#8220;Whiskey Business&#8221; are fueled by wit and wisdom. Lambert collaborated with songwriters and artists including Natalie Hemby, Ashley Monroe, Waylon Payne, Aaron Raitiere and Josh Osborne.

She also offers up a version of Jim Croce&#8217;s &#8220;I&#8217;ll Have to Say I Love You in a Song,&#8221; and on June 26, she will continuing previewing the album with &#8220;Till the Going&#8217;s Gone.&#8221;

“I&#8217;ve always loved every corner of country music,”&nbsp;Lambert &nbsp; said in a statement. “The heartbreak songs, the honky-tonk songs, the songs that make you think and the songs that make you dance. Making this record reminded me that those things don’t have to be separated. We followed the songs wherever they wanted to go, trusted our instincts and ended up with something that feels really fun&nbsp; and&nbsp; really honest to who I am.”

Lambert has already seen seven of her albums debut at No. 1 on Billboard&#8217;s Top Country Albums chart, among them Wildcard and The Weight of These Wings.

Recently, Lambert has also been enjoying heightened success as a cowriter and coproducer on Ella Langley&#8217;s &#8220;Choosin&#8217; Texas,&#8221; which has spent 10 weeks atop the all-genre Billboard Hot 100 chart, and spent 28 weeks at No. 1 on the Hot Country Songs chart.

